1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,720
Your alarm goes off at six thirty.

2
00:00:05,183 --> 00:00:09,343
You check your phone, and your whole day is already cut into little boxes.

3
00:00:09,783 --> 00:00:13,543
Meetings. Deadlines. Lunch at twelve sharp.

4
00:00:13,983 --> 00:00:18,783
Now rewind about nineteen hundred years, to the biggest city in the Western world.

5
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:27,010
Rome, around the year 90 AD. Somewhere between half a million and a million people. Nobody knows exactly.

6
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:29,770
Nobody there owns a clock that ticks.

7
00:00:30,217 --> 00:00:34,697
And yet a Roman poet left us something remarkable: a schedule.

8
00:00:35,150 --> 00:00:40,510
In a short poem, the poet Martial laid out how a day in Rome was spent, hour by hour.

9
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:46,390
So let's borrow his schedule, and live one ordinary day in ancient Rome.

10
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:49,650
From the first hour, to the last.

11
00:00:51,467 --> 00:00:55,627
But first, a problem. What exactly is an hour in Rome?

12
00:00:56,083 --> 00:01:01,363
For the Romans, daytime ran from sunrise to sunset, and it was always divided into twelve hours.

13
00:01:01,817 --> 00:01:04,537
Always twelve. No matter the season.

14
00:01:04,983 --> 00:01:10,663
So in June, when days are long, one Roman hour lasted about seventy-five of our minutes.

15
00:01:11,117 --> 00:01:14,397
In December, it shrank to about forty-five.

16
00:01:14,850 --> 00:01:17,810
Your lunch break literally got shorter in winter.

17
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:21,210
To tell the time, Romans looked at the sun.

18
00:01:21,650 --> 00:01:23,890
And for a long time, they did it badly.

19
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:33,310
According to Pliny the Elder, the first public sundial in the Roman Forum was war loot, brought back from Catania, in Sicily, in 263 BC.

20
00:01:33,750 --> 00:01:38,870
There was one small problem. Catania is much further south than Rome.

21
00:01:39,317 --> 00:01:42,757
So the lines on the dial didn't match the hours in Rome.

22
00:01:43,217 --> 00:01:47,777
Pliny says the Romans kept following that wrong sundial for ninety-nine years.

23
00:01:48,217 --> 00:01:52,217
And not everyone loved sundials, even the accurate ones.

24
00:01:52,683 --> 00:01:58,523
In an old Roman comedy, a hungry character curses the man who invented hours.

25
00:01:58,983 --> 00:02:06,263
"May the gods destroy the man who first discovered the hours," he says, "and who set up a sundial here, and chopped my day into little pieces."

26
00:02:06,717 --> 00:02:10,397
When he was a boy, he says, his belly was his sundial.

27
00:02:10,850 --> 00:02:15,090
He ate whenever he was hungry. Now he eats when the sun allows it.

28
00:02:15,550 --> 00:02:24,510
In 159 BC, Rome finally got a water clock, which measured the hours with flowing water, even at night and on cloudy days.

29
00:02:24,950 --> 00:02:29,590
But most people simply looked at the sky, and listened to the city.

30
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:32,050
And the city was loud.

31
00:02:33,867 --> 00:02:39,067
Our Roman wakes up before sunrise, in a rented room on the third floor of an apartment block.

32
00:02:39,517 --> 00:02:43,437
The Romans called these blocks insulae: islands.

33
00:02:43,883 --> 00:02:47,963
They were tall, crowded, and often badly built.

34
00:02:48,417 --> 00:02:54,657
According to the geographer Strabo, Emperor Augustus limited their height to seventy feet, about twenty-one meters.

35
00:02:55,117 --> 00:02:59,677
The satirist Juvenal joked that Rome was a city held up by thin wooden props.

36
00:03:00,117 --> 00:03:02,997
Collapses and fires were a constant fear.

37
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:05,850
But the everyday enemy was noise.

38
00:03:06,317 --> 00:03:11,837
By law, most wagons were banned from the city streets from sunrise until the last two hours of daylight.

39
00:03:12,283 --> 00:03:15,563
So deliveries rolled through the narrow streets at night.

40
00:03:16,017 --> 00:03:20,097
Juvenal complains that in Rome, a good night's sleep costs a fortune.

41
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:24,390
And that many sick people in the city die simply from lack of sleep.

42
00:03:24,850 --> 00:03:27,410
There is no running water in our Roman's room.

43
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:33,210
Water comes from a public fountain down the street, carried up the stairs in a jar.

44
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:39,650
And the toilet? For many people, a chamber pot. Or a walk to a public latrine.

45
00:03:40,117 --> 00:03:44,517
Getting dressed is quick: a simple wool tunic, belted at the waist.

46
00:03:44,983 --> 00:03:49,063
If he is a citizen with business to do, he also puts on a toga.

47
00:03:49,517 --> 00:03:54,397
A huge, heavy woolen sheet, very hard to drape properly without help.

48
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:58,450
Then he steps out into the street, and into the crowd.

49
00:03:58,917 --> 00:04:04,437
Juvenal describes it: a wave of people in front, a mass of people pushing from behind.

50
00:04:04,883 --> 00:04:12,083
One man jabs you with his elbow, another with a pole. Someone bangs a beam into your head, someone else a wine jar.

51
00:04:12,550 --> 00:04:20,550
Your legs are caked with mud, big feet tread on you from every side, and a soldier's hobnail sticks into your toe.

52
00:04:20,983 --> 00:04:25,543
On his way, he passes the fountain, full of water that has traveled for miles.

53
00:04:25,983 --> 00:04:33,183
Rome was fed by aqueducts, channels that brought fresh water from the hills, mostly underground and on huge arches.

54
00:04:33,650 --> 00:04:37,490
The man in charge of them, Frontinus, was very proud of his job.

55
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:44,590
He wrote: compare these, if you will, with the idle pyramids, or the useless, though famous, works of the Greeks.

56
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:48,090
Roman engineers were not modest people.

57
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:55,020
Breakfast was small: some bread, perhaps with cheese, olives or a little salt.

58
00:04:55,483 --> 00:04:58,283
And many people simply bought it on the street.

59
00:04:58,750 --> 00:05:00,990
Martial wrote a two-line poem about it.

60
00:05:01,450 --> 00:05:07,610
"Get up! The baker is already selling breakfast to the boys, and the crested birds of dawn are singing everywhere."

61
00:05:08,050 --> 00:05:10,290
Those boys were on their way to school.

62
00:05:10,750 --> 00:05:13,550
And school started early. Very early.

63
00:05:14,017 --> 00:05:17,057
Martial lived near a schoolmaster, and he hated him.

64
00:05:17,517 --> 00:05:24,717
"The crested roosters haven't even broken the silence," he wrote, "and you're already thundering with savage shouting and beatings."

65
00:05:25,183 --> 00:05:29,583
Lessons often happened in a shop or under a porch, open to the street.

66
00:05:30,050 --> 00:05:32,450
So the whole neighborhood heard every mistake.

67
00:05:34,267 --> 00:05:38,187
Now the first hour begins. And Martial tells us exactly what happens.

68
00:05:38,650 --> 00:05:41,930
"The first and second hour wear out the people paying their morning calls."

69
00:05:42,383 --> 00:05:45,263
This was the salutatio, the morning greeting.

70
00:05:45,717 --> 00:05:48,917
Rome ran on a system of patrons and clients.

71
00:05:49,383 --> 00:05:54,263
A rich patron protected his clients, and helped them with money, jobs and legal trouble.

72
00:05:54,717 --> 00:05:59,117
In return, the clients gave him something every rich Roman wanted: a crowd.

73
00:05:59,583 --> 00:06:03,823
So every morning, clients in their togas lined up at his door to wish him good morning.

74
00:06:04,283 --> 00:06:07,883
The bigger the crowd at your door, the more important you looked.

75
00:06:08,350 --> 00:06:13,630
The clients were often rewarded with a small handout, called the sportula: the little basket.

76
00:06:14,083 --> 00:06:18,483
Originally it was food. By Martial's time, it was usually cash.

77
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:23,350
Martial and Juvenal both name the amount: a hundred quadrantes.

78
00:06:23,817 --> 00:06:28,937
The quadrans was the smallest copper coin in Rome. Keep it in mind. It will come back later.

79
00:06:29,383 --> 00:06:34,023
After the greeting, the clients might follow their patron to the Forum, like a walking advertisement.

80
00:06:34,483 --> 00:06:38,563
Martial spent years doing this, and complained about it constantly.

81
00:06:40,367 --> 00:06:44,607
"The third hour," says Martial, "keeps the hoarse lawyers busy."

82
00:06:45,050 --> 00:06:49,450
Trials were held in the open, in the forums and in the great basilicas.

83
00:06:49,917 --> 00:06:53,197
And the speeches were timed. With water clocks.

84
00:06:53,650 --> 00:07:00,930
Pliny the Younger, a lawyer and senator, wrote that in one big trial before the Senate he spoke for almost five hours.

85
00:07:01,383 --> 00:07:06,903
He had been given twelve water clocks of speaking time, and four more were added.

86
00:07:07,350 --> 00:07:10,470
Big trials drew crowds, like a show.

87
00:07:10,917 --> 00:07:15,957
Pliny also complained that some speakers hired people to sit in the audience and applaud.

88
00:07:16,417 --> 00:07:20,497
Paid applause. Two thousand years before the laugh track.

89
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:27,420
"Until the fifth hour," Martial continues, "Rome stretches out her many kinds of work."

90
00:07:27,883 --> 00:07:30,763
And most Romans did work. Hard.

91
00:07:31,217 --> 00:07:35,617
The city was full of tabernae: small shops opening straight onto the street.

92
00:07:36,083 --> 00:07:40,483
Often, the family that ran the shop lived in a room above it or behind it.

93
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:47,270
Bakers, butchers, shoemakers, barbers, blacksmiths, all side by side.

94
00:07:47,717 --> 00:07:51,317
The barber's shop was a place to sit, shave and catch up on the gossip.

95
00:07:51,783 --> 00:07:57,623
But Roman barbers had no fine steel safety razors, and some were famously slow.

96
00:07:58,083 --> 00:08:04,803
Martial wrote about one barber who worked so slowly that, by the time he'd gone round his client's face, a new beard had grown.

97
00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:10,210
Behind much of this work were people who never appear in Martial's schedule.

98
00:08:10,650 --> 00:08:16,090
Many of the cooks, porters, bath attendants and shop workers of Rome were enslaved.

99
00:08:16,550 --> 00:08:19,590
Their day ended when someone else decided it did.

100
00:08:20,050 --> 00:08:25,330
Some of the busiest workers were the fullers, who washed and finished wool cloth.

101
00:08:25,783 --> 00:08:28,103
Their secret ingredient was urine.

102
00:08:28,550 --> 00:08:34,230
It was collected in jars, then workers standing in tubs stomped it into the cloth with their feet.

103
00:08:34,683 --> 00:08:39,163
Urine was so valuable that Emperor Vespasian put a tax on it.

104
00:08:39,617 --> 00:08:42,097
His son Titus found this disgusting.

105
00:08:42,550 --> 00:08:51,030
So, according to the historian Suetonius, Vespasian held a coin from the first payment under his son's nose, and asked if it smelled.

106
00:08:51,483 --> 00:08:53,003
Titus said no.

107
00:08:53,450 --> 00:08:56,570
"And yet," said Vespasian, "it comes from urine."

108
00:08:57,017 --> 00:09:00,457
Lunch was quick and light, often eaten standing up.

109
00:09:00,917 --> 00:09:03,317
And for that, there were street bars.

110
00:09:03,783 --> 00:09:11,303
In Pompeii, a small town south of Rome buried by Vesuvius in 79 AD, archaeologists have found many of these counters.

111
00:09:11,750 --> 00:09:15,990
In 2020, they finished excavating one that still had its bright paintings.

112
00:09:16,450 --> 00:09:21,490
Ducks, a rooster, a dog, and a sea nymph riding a sea horse.

113
00:09:21,950 --> 00:09:28,670
In the jars set into the counter, they found the remains of duck, pig, goat, fish and snails.

114
00:09:29,117 --> 00:09:32,397
Fast food, nearly two thousand years ago.

115
00:09:32,850 --> 00:09:37,570
And on almost everything, Romans poured garum: a sauce made from fermented fish.

116
00:09:38,017 --> 00:09:43,777
Pliny the Elder describes it as fish guts and leftover parts, soaked in salt until they turned to liquid.

117
00:09:44,217 --> 00:09:50,297
Sounds terrible. But modern fish sauce is made in a very similar way, and you might have some in your kitchen.

118
00:09:50,750 --> 00:09:52,670
And not every morning was the same.

119
00:09:53,117 --> 00:09:59,117
Their official calendar didn't run on our seven-day week. It ran on a market cycle of eight days.

120
00:09:59,583 --> 00:10:03,423
Every eighth day was the nundinae, market day.

121
00:10:03,883 --> 00:10:08,203
Farmers came into town to sell their produce, and to hear about new laws.

122
00:10:08,650 --> 00:10:11,930
Instead of Monday, the Romans had market day.

123
00:10:13,733 --> 00:10:17,333
"The sixth hour," says Martial, "is rest for the tired."

124
00:10:17,783 --> 00:10:22,743
Around midday, the city slowed down. People ate, and many took a nap.

125
00:10:23,183 --> 00:10:26,863
And that habit left a mark that still exists today.

126
00:10:27,317 --> 00:10:30,277
The sixth hour, in Latin, is sexta hora.

127
00:10:30,717 --> 00:10:33,357
In Spanish, it became siesta.

128
00:10:35,167 --> 00:10:37,567
"The seventh," Martial says, "will be the end."

129
00:10:38,017 --> 00:10:41,377
For many Romans, the working day was over by early afternoon.

130
00:10:41,817 --> 00:10:45,577
Time to walk around, meet friends, and read the walls.

131
00:10:46,017 --> 00:10:48,017
Because Roman walls talked.

132
00:10:48,483 --> 00:10:54,483
In Pompeii alone, archaeologists have recorded thousands of painted notices and scratched graffiti.

133
00:10:54,950 --> 00:10:58,630
There were election posters, painted in big red letters.

134
00:10:59,083 --> 00:11:05,083
One candidate, Marcus Cerrinius Vatia, was supported by "all the late drinkers."

135
00:11:05,550 --> 00:11:08,430
Another poster for him came from "the little thieves."

136
00:11:08,883 --> 00:11:12,403
Some historians suspect his rivals painted those ones.

137
00:11:12,850 --> 00:11:14,850
There were visitors leaving their mark.

138
00:11:15,317 --> 00:11:20,837
"Gaius Pumidius Dipilus was here," dated to the third of October, 78 BC.

139
00:11:21,283 --> 00:11:26,483
There was a very honest review, scratched on a wall by a doorway, and addressed to the host.

140
00:11:26,950 --> 00:11:33,670
"We wet the bed, host. I admit it, we did wrong. If you want to know why: there was no chamber pot."

141
00:11:34,117 --> 00:11:39,477
And there was one graffito, found in several places in Pompeii, that says it all.

142
00:11:39,917 --> 00:11:45,117
"I'm amazed, O wall, that you haven't collapsed under the weight of so many boring writers."

143
00:11:46,933 --> 00:11:52,053
"The eighth hour, until the ninth, belongs to the oiled-up wrestling grounds," says Martial.

144
00:11:52,517 --> 00:11:54,997
In other words: it's bath time.

145
00:11:55,450 --> 00:11:58,570
The public baths were the heart of Roman social life.

146
00:11:59,017 --> 00:12:02,377
And they were cheap. Entry could cost a single quadrans.

147
00:12:02,817 --> 00:12:07,617
Remember the hundred coins from the morning handout? One of them buys you a bath.

148
00:12:08,083 --> 00:12:13,683
First, exercise: running, wrestling, ball games, lifting weights.

149
00:12:14,150 --> 00:12:18,070
Then the rooms: the warm room, the hot room, and the cold pool.

150
00:12:18,517 --> 00:12:22,757
And who went to the baths? Almost everyone. Men and women.

151
00:12:23,217 --> 00:12:26,577
In many baths, they went at different times.

152
00:12:27,017 --> 00:12:32,297
A bronze tablet from a Roman mining town in what is now Portugal lists the rules of its baths.

153
00:12:32,750 --> 00:12:38,110
Women bathe from the first hour to the seventh. Men from the eighth hour until two hours after dark.

154
00:12:38,550 --> 00:12:44,310
And the price? Men paid half an as. Women paid a whole as. Double.

155
00:12:44,750 --> 00:12:46,830
There was no soap as we know it.

156
00:12:47,283 --> 00:12:52,403
Romans covered themselves in oil and scraped it off with a curved metal tool called a strigil.

157
00:12:52,850 --> 00:12:56,850
Oil, sweat and dirt, all in one go.

158
00:12:57,317 --> 00:12:59,237
And the baths were loud.

159
00:12:59,683 --> 00:13:04,883
We know exactly how loud, because the philosopher Seneca once lived right above one.

160
00:13:05,350 --> 00:13:07,510
He described the noise in a letter.

161
00:13:07,950 --> 00:13:10,590
The grunts of men lifting lead weights.

162
00:13:11,050 --> 00:13:14,410
The slap of the masseur's hands on someone's shoulders.

163
00:13:14,850 --> 00:13:17,970
A ball player counting his score out loud.

164
00:13:18,417 --> 00:13:22,177
The man who sings in the bath, because he loves the sound of his own voice.

165
00:13:22,617 --> 00:13:25,337
The splash of people jumping into the pool.

166
00:13:25,783 --> 00:13:31,863
The hair-plucker, shrieking to attract customers, and only going quiet when he made someone else scream instead.

167
00:13:32,317 --> 00:13:37,837
And the sellers of sausages, pastries and drinks, each with his own special cry.

168
00:13:38,283 --> 00:13:41,963
Seneca insisted that a true philosopher could ignore all of this.

169
00:13:42,417 --> 00:13:46,897
And then, at the end of the very same letter, he admits he's moving out.

170
00:13:47,350 --> 00:13:54,790
The baths also had public latrines: long rows of stone seats, side by side, with no walls between them.

171
00:13:55,250 --> 00:14:00,850
Instead of toilet paper, Seneca mentions a sponge on a stick, kept in the latrine for wiping.

172
00:14:01,317 --> 00:14:02,917
Quite possibly shared.

173
00:14:04,733 --> 00:14:09,213
"The ninth hour," Martial writes, "orders us to crush the piled-up couches."

174
00:14:09,650 --> 00:14:14,210
Dinner time. The cena, the main meal of the day.

175
00:14:14,650 --> 00:14:21,850
In wealthy homes, guests lay on couches arranged around a low table, three to a couch, leaning on the left elbow.

176
00:14:22,317 --> 00:14:24,717
A Roman dinner could last for hours.

177
00:14:25,183 --> 00:14:29,343
The Romans even had an expression for a full meal: from the egg to the apples.

178
00:14:29,783 --> 00:14:34,103
Eggs to start. Fruit to finish. And everything in between.

179
00:14:34,550 --> 00:14:37,830
And Roman hosts took it personally when you didn't show up.

180
00:14:38,283 --> 00:14:43,883
Pliny the Younger once wrote a mock-angry letter to a friend who accepted his dinner invitation and never came.

181
00:14:44,350 --> 00:14:52,270
He listed everything that went to waste: a lettuce each, three snails, two eggs, olives, beets, onions.

182
00:14:52,717 --> 00:14:57,117
And entertainment: a reader, an actor, or a lyre player.

183
00:14:57,583 --> 00:15:04,063
Instead, the friend went to another house for oysters, sea urchins and Spanish dancers.

184
00:15:04,517 --> 00:15:07,317
Honestly, it's hard to blame him.

185
00:15:07,783 --> 00:15:13,623
And in one Roman novel, a ridiculously rich man called Trimalchio has a clock in his dining room.

186
00:15:14,083 --> 00:15:18,003
And a trumpeter, to announce how much of his life he has just lost.

187
00:15:18,450 --> 00:15:22,610
Even in fiction, the Romans were a little obsessed with time.

188
00:15:23,050 --> 00:15:25,530
But most Romans were not rich.

189
00:15:25,983 --> 00:15:33,423
Many apartments had no real kitchen. Fire on the third floor of a building held up by props was a terrible idea.

190
00:15:33,883 --> 00:15:41,083
So ordinary people ate simply: porridge, bread, beans, vegetables, and cheap wine mixed with water.

191
00:15:41,550 --> 00:15:44,510
Or a hot meal from the street bar on the corner.

192
00:15:44,950 --> 00:15:48,470
And the tenth hour? Martial has a special plan for it.

193
00:15:48,917 --> 00:15:52,837
"The tenth hour," he writes, "is the hour for my little books."

194
00:15:53,283 --> 00:15:58,883
He's hoping the emperor will read his poems at dinner, relaxed, with a cup of wine in his hand.

195
00:15:59,350 --> 00:16:03,510
A whole poem about the Roman day, and it ends with a sales pitch.

196
00:16:03,950 --> 00:16:05,870
Some things never change.

197
00:16:07,667 --> 00:16:13,027
But this was an ordinary working day. And Rome had a lot of days that were not ordinary.

198
00:16:13,483 --> 00:16:20,203
Dozens of days a year were public festivals, many with free shows paid for by the state or by rich politicians.

199
00:16:20,650 --> 00:16:23,770
Plays at the theater. Gladiators at the amphitheater.

200
00:16:24,217 --> 00:16:28,857
And the biggest show of all: the chariot races at the Circus Maximus.

201
00:16:29,317 --> 00:16:35,237
Ancient writers say it held somewhere between a hundred and fifty and two hundred and fifty thousand spectators.

202
00:16:35,683 --> 00:16:40,883
The fans were divided into teams, by color: the Reds, the Whites, the Greens and the Blues.

203
00:16:41,350 --> 00:16:48,230
Not everyone was a fan. Pliny the Younger spent race days at home, writing, and was proud of it.

204
00:16:48,683 --> 00:16:54,603
He couldn't understand why thousands of grown men wanted to watch horses run, again and again.

205
00:16:55,050 --> 00:17:00,170
They don't even care about the horses, he wrote. They cheer for a piece of cloth.

206
00:17:00,617 --> 00:17:05,737
If the drivers swapped colors in the middle of the race, the fans would swap with them.

207
00:17:06,183 --> 00:17:07,783
Sound familiar?

208
00:17:08,250 --> 00:17:10,410
Juvenal had a darker view.

209
00:17:10,850 --> 00:17:14,290
The Roman people, he wrote, used to hand out armies and offices.

210
00:17:14,750 --> 00:17:19,310
Now they only long for two things: bread, and circuses.

211
00:17:21,100 --> 00:17:23,020
And then, the sun goes down.

212
00:17:23,483 --> 00:17:25,723
Rome has no street lights.

213
00:17:26,183 --> 00:17:31,303
The streets are pitch dark. Rich men walk home with slaves carrying torches.

214
00:17:31,750 --> 00:17:36,070
Juvenal says you'd be foolish to go out to dinner without first writing your will.

215
00:17:36,517 --> 00:17:41,077
Because from any open window above you, someone might throw out a broken pot.

216
00:17:41,517 --> 00:17:43,357
Or empty something much worse.

217
00:17:43,817 --> 00:17:48,137
At night, the streets belong to the wagons, the drunks, and the fires.

218
00:17:48,583 --> 00:17:53,703
Augustus created a corps of watchmen, the vigiles, to patrol the dark streets and fight fires.

219
00:17:54,150 --> 00:17:59,430
Up in his small room, our Roman lights a clay oil lamp, for a little while.

220
00:17:59,883 --> 00:18:03,083
Lamp oil costs money, so the evening is short.

221
00:18:03,550 --> 00:18:07,390
He goes to sleep. Down in the street, the wagons start rolling again.

222
00:18:07,850 --> 00:18:11,850
And in a few hours, before the roosters, the schoolmaster will start shouting.

223
00:18:13,667 --> 00:18:15,907
So, what did ancient Romans do all day?

224
00:18:16,350 --> 00:18:18,350
They worked, mostly in the morning.

225
00:18:18,817 --> 00:18:20,977
They networked, every single morning.

226
00:18:21,417 --> 00:18:26,537
They ate, gossiped, wrote on walls, and went to the baths, almost every afternoon.

227
00:18:26,983 --> 00:18:29,863
And a surprising part of their day is still with us.

228
00:18:30,317 --> 00:18:34,557
The English word noon comes from the Latin nona hora: the ninth hour.

229
00:18:35,017 --> 00:18:39,897
It once meant mid-afternoon, and over the centuries it slid back to midday.

230
00:18:40,350 --> 00:18:43,070
Siesta comes from the sixth hour.

231
00:18:43,517 --> 00:18:47,997
And that hungry man in the Roman comedy, cursing the inventor of the sundial?

232
00:18:48,450 --> 00:18:52,290
He sounds a lot like us, staring at a calendar full of meetings.

233
00:18:52,750 --> 00:18:58,670
All the sources for this video, from Martial to Seneca to the walls of Pompeii, are in the description.

234
00:18:59,117 --> 00:19:03,037
And tell me in the comments: which Roman hour would you want to live in?
